<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 2 
\section{Bus Protocol}
Low speed: 1.5Mb / sec

Full speed: 12.5 Mb/sec (8x faster)

(we'll ignore low speed mode ...)
%%%%** Section 2.1
\subsection{Frames and Polling}
Time is divided into 1ms ``Frames''.
Each frame time the Host transmits a ``Start of Frame"
(SOF)  packet.

%%%%** Section 2.1.1 
\subsubsection{SOF packet structure}

\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
SYNC  & PID & Frame Number (11bits) & CRC (5bits) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

Frame Number rolls over.

%%%%** Section 2.1.2 
\subsubsection{PID}
{\bf ALL} packets have a packet ID (PID)


\begin{tabular}{|c|c| }
\hline
PID (4 bits) & $\overline{\mathrm {PID}}$ (4 bits)\\
\hline
\end{tabular}


The 4-bit PID is repeated, complemented, inside the PID
byte for error detection.

32 PID values including: \hspace{0.25in}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline
Type	& Code \\ \hline
ACK	& 0010 \\ \hline
NAK	& 1010 \\ \hline
IN	& 1001 \\ \hline
OUT	& 0001 \\ \hline
SOF	& 0101 \\ \hline
DATA0	& 0011 \\ \hline
DATA1	& 1011 \\ \hline
\end{tabular}

{\tt TOKEN} packets are for {\tt SOF}, polling,  and pipe control

%%%%** Section 2.1.3 
\subsubsection{Polling Sequence}

\begin{enumerate}
     \item Host sends ``token packet''  \\
     \begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
       \hline
        PID  &  ADDR (7 bits)  & Endpoint (4 bits) & CRC (5 bits)\\
       \hline
     \end{tabular}

     PID: {\tt OUT (0001)} = host asks endpoint to receive data.\\
     {\tt IN (1001)} = host asks endpoint to send data.

     \item source sends data packet to destination\\
     \begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
       \hline
        PID  & DATA (0-1023 bytes)  &  CRC (16 bits)\\
       \hline
     \end{tabular}


     \item destination sends handshake packet (ACK/NAK) \\
    \begin{tabular}{|c|}
       \hline
        PID  \\
       \hline
     \end{tabular}

\end{enumerate}

There can be many transactions in each frame.   10\%
of each frame is reserved for Control packets.

%%%%** Section 2.2
\subsection{Pipes}
Connections are called ``pipes''.  Attributes of pipes:
\begin{itemize}
     \item bandwidth
     \item data flow  type
     \item endpoint characteristics (direction, buffer
     size).
\end{itemize}

Pipes are established when a device is plugged in and (auto)
configured.

``Default Control Pipe" always exists for each device to
set and query status and control of device.

%%%%** Section 2.3
\subsection{Data Flow Types}
Each pipe can be one of the following types
\begin{enumerate}
        \item Control Transfer. configure the device when
        it is attached.  Other control functions.
        \item Bulk Data Transfer. non-realtime transfer
        of big file.  Example: Printer
        \item Interrupt Data Transfer.  Small data amounts
        with short latency requirements. Example: Mouse
        \item Isochronous Data Transfer. Fixed (negotiated)
        bandwidth amount with constant latency. Example:
        Audio.
\end{enumerate}

%%%%** Section 2.3.1 
\subsubsection{Characteristics of Data Flow Types}
\begin{enumerate}
	\item Packet format
	\item Direction of flow
	\item Packet size (or size ranges)
	\item Bus Access constraints
	\item Latency Constraints
	\item Error Handling
\end{enumerate}

%%%%** Section 2.3.2 
\subsubsection{Control Transfer}
10\% of the bus bandwidth is reserved for control transfers.

%%%%** Section 2.3.3 
\subsubsection{Bulk}
Low priority in the sense that Interrupt and Isochronus
get the bandwidth first, Bulk gets what is left.

No data format requirement.

Max packet size is 8, 16, 32, or 64 bytes.

Packet Overhead 

\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c| }
\hline
SYNC (3)  & PID (3) & Endpoint & CRC (3)   \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

Counts () indicate total for three transactions:

\begin{enumerate}
	\item Poll (host to function)
	\item Data (either direction) \\
	
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
SYNC    & PID   & Data & CRC (2)   \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

	\item {\tt ACK/NAK}
\end{enumerate}

Total overhead, 13 bytes packet header, 3 byte delay between
packets.

Access granted as bandwidth is available.

If errors are detected, transfers are re-tried.

Guaranteed data delivery. No bandwidth or latency guarantee.

Transfer completion is detected when endpoint: \\
\hspace{0.5in}Has sent all bytes expected \\
\hspace{0.5in}Sends a packet with less than max payload size.

%%%%** Section 2.3.4 
\subsubsection{Interrupt}
typically keystrokes and mouse movements.

Endpoints specify max packet size when configured.

Negotiated based on min(source,dest,bus avail).

Max packet size 64 bytes, max 108 transactions per frame.

A mouse typically sends packets only when moved by user.
Each packet consists of a sync byte, and one byte each
for $\Delta$X, $\Delta$Y, and button/wheel status.

%%%%** Section 2.3.5 
\subsubsection{Isochronus}

Periodic bursts of data at a pre-set rate.   Host
makes sure bus is available (quiet) at scheduled
times for this transfer.

Endpoint specifies packet size when it is configured.

Packet overhead:

\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c| }
\hline
SYNC (2)  & PID (2) & Endpoint & CRC (3)   \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

Total overhead, 8 bytes packet header, 1 byte delay between
packets.

No {\it length} field in header but length can vary(!)

Payload is up to 1023 bytes.  max 150 1-byte transfers
per frame.

1 packet per frame per endpoint.

Because latency must be maintained low, there is
no time for {\tt ACK/NAK} and re-transmission.

Hardware error rate is expected to be very low.
But 0 error rate is not expected in this mode.

%%%%** Section 2.4
\subsection{Devices}

Devices are divided into classes (similar to C++ idea).

Devices must be able to {\it report} info on their identity
and configuration.

\begin{itemize}
        \item Standard Info: All USB devices:
        vendor ID, Class, Pwr Mgt., endpoints.

        USB Control and status info.

        \item Class Info: same for all devices of
        the same class.  Example: Specific info for
        keyboards.

        \item Vendor: Proprietary info for the
        device vendor.  Example: Firmware revision
        number.

\end{itemize}
Devices must support endpoint 0 for Default Control Pipe.

<s>\end{slide}

<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 2.5
\subsection{Speed}
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|r|}
\hline
Type	&  Payload Size	& Frame Utilization & Max bytes/sec \\ \hline
\hline
Bulk	&  16		& 2\%		    & 816,000	    \\ \hline
Bulk	&  64		& 5\%		    & 1,216,000    \\ \hline
Interrupt & 4		& 1\%		    & 352,000    \\ \hline
Isochronus & 16		& 2\%		    & 960,000    \\ \hline
Isochronus & 512	& 35\%		    & 1,024,000 \\ \hline

\end{tabular}

{\bf Notes:}

 1,000,000 bytes / sec = 8 MBaud(!)

 Isochronus --- only 1 transfer per frame.  BW above shows {\it total }
 for ALL Isochronus connections.


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 3 
\section{Electrical}
Four wires:
\begin{enumerate}
        \item V{\sc BUS} \\
        +5V DC power.  May power devices.
        \item D+  Data line 1
        \item D-  Data line 2
        \item GND
\end{enumerate}
Software support for power management (suspend/resume)

%%%%** Section 3.1
\subsection{Robustness}

\begin{itemize}
        \item differential line drivers and shielding
        \item CRC protection of all parts of packet.
        (1 and 2 bit errors 100\% detected).
        \item timeouts for lost packets --- self recovery
        \item Flow control for low priority streams
        prevents buffer overflow/underflow.
\end{itemize}

%%%%** Section 3.2
\subsection{Attachment}

Devices can be plugged in to hub at any time. Hub
has status bits which let host know which ports have
devices plugged in.

\begin{itemize}
        \item host queries hub
        \item hub reports status bits
        \item if host detects a new device, it
        \item assigns a unique USB address to the new
        port.
        \item sends a control packet to enable the port.
        \item if the new device is a hub, repeat process
        for new hub.
        \item if new device is a function, notify software
        driver on host that a new instance of this function
        has been attached to the bus.
\end{itemize}



<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide} 
%%%%** Section 3.3
\subsection{Voltage Levels and Rise Times:}
<*chn>
\begin{center}
   \pdftooltip{\includegraphics[height=2in]{usb_figs/00078.eps}}{*\#*\#Guess by Claude.ai: USB electrical signal voltage level diagram (Figure 7-1) showing driver output thresholds (VOL, VOH) and receiver input thresholds (VIL, VIH)}   \\
(Figure 7-1, page 107)
\end{center}


%<s>\vspace{-2in}
<s>  \scalebox{.15}{  \pdftooltip{\includegraphics{usb_figs/00078.eps}}{*\#*\#Guess by Claude.ai: USB electrical signal voltage level diagram (Figure 7-1) showing driver output thresholds (VOL, VOH) and receiver input thresholds (VIL, VIH)} }\\


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}


<*>
Driver Low, V{\sc ol},  must be $<$ 0.3v (1.5k$\Omega$ to 3.6V)

Driver High, V{\sc oh}, must be $>$ 2.8v (15k$\Omega$ to 0V)

Receiver High, V{\sc ih}, $>$ 2.0V must be detected as High

Receiver Low, V{\sc il}, $<$ 0.8V must be detected as Low

Worst case inputs: [-1.0V --- +4.6V]

90$\Omega$ characteristic impedance (high speed)

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 3.4
\subsection{Differential Drive} 
<s> \scalebox{0.15}{
<chn>\scalebox{0.15}{
    \pdftooltip{\includegraphics{usb_figs/00079.eps}}{*\#*\#Guess by Claude.ai: USB differential drive circuit diagram (Figure 7-2) showing D+ and D- signal lines driven differentially to eliminate common-mode noise, with output enable (OE) control}
   }

(Fig 7-2 page 108)

D+ and D- usually signal by their {\it difference}

This eliminates common mode noise

{\tt OE} enables the output drivers.

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 3.5
\subsection{Signaling Levels (high speed)}

Differential ``1'':  (D+ $-$ D-) $>$ 200mV

Differential ``0'':  (D- $-$ D+) $>$ 200mV

Idle: Differential ``1''

Start of Packet (SOP):  Idle $\to$ Differential ``0''

(``J" and ``K" states (!))

Reset:   D+ and D- $<$ 0.8V for $>=$ 10 ms.

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 4 
\section{Modulation and Channel Coding}
%%%%** Section 4.1
\subsection{NRZI}

Non Return to Zero:

Logic zero is sent by toggling

Logic one is sent by not toggling

{\tt Data} $\to$ {\tt NRZI Signal }

{\tt
00000000 $\to$  01010101

11111111 $\to$  11111111
}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 4.2
\subsection{Bit Stuffing}

Packets don't have as many start and stop bits as RS-232

Receiver needs to see 1$\to$0 or 0$\to$1 transitions at
least every seven bits.

Bit-stuffing inserts a zero whenever there are 6 ones
in a row.

{\tt
11111111 $\to$  111111011
}

Now the receiver sees at least a few transitions for {\it any}
data stream and so it can keep its clock phase locked.

Receiver hardware automatically removes stuffed bits.

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 4.3
\subsection{Update: USB 2.0 }

Released April 2000.

Logically the same as 1.1, backwards compatible.

Provides additional speed mode:  {\bf Hi-Speed}  480 Mbit/s

Caution: if a device is labeled ``Full Speed USB 2.0" you are really getting USB 1.1 (!)

Only ``High Speed" or ``480 Mbit/s" is a ``real" USB 2.0 device.

<s>\end{slide}

